@@ -0,0 +1,48 @@
+Feature: the **plan play** is the second play of the shaping loop (ADR 049). It runs right after `/shape-slice`, with no stop between them. It makes the plan for the business intent: the plan file, one epic for the slice and one story for each feature. It writes nothing in the evals folder: /shape-slice writes the slice's hidden file (ontology v11, ADR 052). Each story carries what an agent needs to build it, and the plan can be tracked.
+
+## Done when
+- A new play (built with `/create-play`) takes the slice `/shape-slice` wrote and writes `.garura/project/plans/<business intent>.yaml` — the one plan for that intent; a second slice for the same intent joins the same plan (ontology Q30).
+- It makes, in the tracker, one epic for the slice, and one story for each feature of the slice as a sub-issue of the epic.
+- Each story holds the goal, the limits, the Context guidance and the expectations from its feature's ICE and its capability's ICE, and never a failure or recovery condition (ontology v11, ADR 049 §4, ADR 052).
+- The epic keeps the feature-to-story map: an entry for each feature, with the story that delivers it. It agrees with the plan's Tree.
+- It finds the epic and stories that exist before it makes new ones. A second run makes no duplicates.
+- When a feature leaves the slice after its plan exists, its story moves out of the plan; the plan play does not delete it, and the story stays in the tracker. A feature that comes in gets one story (the product owner's answer Q-change-1, /shape-slice run #866).
+- The epic is in the tracker only; the spine `epics` list and `epic.md` pages leave the product model.
+
+## Not in this issue
+- The eval (#798) and the one checkpoint for the loop (#796).
+- Building the epic (the build-stage business intent).
+
+## Links
+- Serves: #788
+- Part of: #794
+- Needs: #795
+
+## Scope of work
+
+Goals:
+- When the product owner runs /shape-plan on a business intent, the intent's one plan file (.garura/project/plans/<intent>.yaml) exists, with one epic for each slice that serves the intent and one story for each feature of that slice.
+- The plan names every capability and feature it holds, each with its ICE.
+- A second run finds the plan, the epics and the stories that exist, and makes no copies of them.
+
+Limits:
+- Write only the plan file, the epics and the stories.
+- Each story holds the goals, the limits, the expectations and the Context guidance from its feature's ICE and that feature's capability's ICE, and nothing from the hidden file.
+- The hidden file and all that it holds belong to /shape-slice (decision 6); the eval belongs to /shape-eval.
+- Nothing goes to the tracker before the product owner types yes.
+- Do not pick, rank or test slices; the product owner chose the slice at /shape-slice.
+- Do not wait for a full product model; write a missing model page as a light page during the run.
+
+Expectations:
+- The product owner runs /shape-plan 788 in Claude Code after /shape-slice and finds the plan file, one epic, and one story for each feature of the slice.
+- No story holds any line from the hidden file.
+- Building the plans for #717 and its partner gives the same trees that were filed by hand on 2026-10-21.
+
+## Context guidance
+
+- Read: the slice in the spine, and the ICE of each feature and of its capability, once for each story
+- Never read: the slice's hidden file and the evals folder
+- Persona: the product owner, who runs and tracks the plan; the build play reads the stories after
+- In and out: in: the plan file in .garura/project/plans/ and the tracker's epics and stories; out: picking the slice, the hidden file, the eval and the build (not built yet, #818)
+
+Represents: fn-818
diff --git a/.garura/project/issues/798/context/shape-eval/inputs/story-898.md b/.garura/project/issues/798/context/shape-eval/inputs/story-898.md
--- /dev/null
+++ b/.garura/project/issues/798/context/shape-eval/inputs/story-898.md
@@ -0,0 +1,51 @@
+Feature: when agents build, they build for the business intent, not only to pass the eval.
+
+The product owner, 2026-10-26, in the design of #798: "all of these things, constraints, the setting in the product model, all in plain text. If an agent really wants to go back and read it, it can go back and read everything. The point we're trying to make is that once the slice happens, we need an eval, and that eval is what we need to pass. I just want to make sure that when the agents are building, they don't only think through the evals, but they look at the intent and build for that." It replaces #828 (encryption, dropped with reason).
+
+## What it does
+
+- **The build is given the intent, never the eval.** A build's inputs are the slice's stories, each with its scope of work written from the ICE (goals, limits, expectations) and its Context guidance. No build play, build skill or build helper is handed the eval or the hidden file, or their folder. Nothing is hidden: the eval is simply not part of the brief.
+- **A check flags any build that read the evals folder.** Each play run already records its session (the session stamp and its ledger). After a build, a check reads that session's ledger and flags any read of `plans/<plan>/evals/`. It does not block; it makes the shortcut visible.
+- **The eval measures the intent.** The eval play (#798) writes checks that test, from the outside through the usage guide, what the business intent promises, so passing the eval and meeting the intent are the same thing.
+
+## Done when
+
+- No build play's inputs (contracts, context files) name the evals folder, the eval or the hidden file; a lint check fails one that does.
+- A check over a build session's ledger flags a read of the evals folder, with a test that shows it flags one and passes a clean session.
+- The ontology and `managing-work.md` say the build is given the intent, never the eval (not "the eval is encrypted").
+
+## Where it sits
+
+- Under epic #794, the epic of slice `sl-intent-to-design`. `/shape-slice` adds its feature to the slice, with its ICE, in the change for #798.
+
+## Not in this story
+
+- Blocking reads by hook (#893, the guardrails).
+- Encryption (#828, dropped).
+
+## Scope of work
+
+Goals:
+- No build play, build skill, build agent or context sub-agent that a build starts is given the eval, the hidden file or the plan's evals folder.
+- A build is given the business intent through the slice's stories.
+- Each build session that read the plan's evals folder has a flag in that build run's evidence that names the read.
+- Each slice has one eval that says, from the outside, if its business intent is met on the slice's surface.
+
+Limits:
+- The check flags a read and never blocks a build.
+- Only /shape-slice, /shape-eval and the eval may name the evals folder.
+- The eval play reads the epic, its stories and the hidden file, never an ICE page, the usage guide or the code, and writes only in the plan's evals folder.
+
+Expectations:
+- The lint check runs over the build plays and stops a build play whose inputs name the evals folder.
+- The test of the session-ledger check flags, in the evidence, one session that read the evals folder, and passes a clean session.
+- After /shape-plan, /shape-eval writes the eval in the plan's evals folder, and the inputs of no build play name that folder.
+
+## Context guidance
+
+- Read: The build plays' inputs, and a build session's ledger, found through the session stamp in the run's evidence.
+- Never read: The eval, the hidden file and the rest of the plan's evals folder.
+- Persona: The product owner, who trusts an eval pass only when the build aimed at the intent.
+- In and out: In: the flag in the build run's evidence and the lint check over the build plays. Out: blocking by a hook (#893), encryption (#828, dropped) and the drift checks and evidence warnings in Griffin.
+
+Represents: fn-898
