+# Design draft: #908, `/shape-slice --edit`, with the shared part of #905
+
+Status: draft for the product owner; nothing is built. 2026-10-27.
+
+## What the person gets
+
+`/shape-slice #788 --edit: <their words>` changes named parts of the slice that already exists, and
+nothing else:
+- the slice record: its name, outcome, or the tester's action;
+- the slice's hidden file: the "when", "way back" or "who fixes" of named failure lines;
+- a part's spine one-line (this takes in the earlier rule that a fill may replace a one-line).
+
+## How a run goes (a separate edit path in the play)
+
+1. start-change opens the change, as today.
+2. The play reads the records the slice has (the slice record, the hidden file, the spine entries).
+3. A skill turns the person's words into an exact list: for each change, the file, the part (for
+   example "hidden file, fn-818, F8, who fixes"), the old text and the new text. The skill never
+   changes anything; it only writes the list.
+4. The brief shows the list: each changed line, old and new, and each other record the edit makes
+   out of date (for example, an eval scenario that tests that line), named but not changed.
+5. The pinned checkpoint: yes, change or stop. Nothing is written before the yes.
+6. A script applies exactly the list, and refuses a line whose old text no longer matches.
+7. A check compares each record before and after and refuses any change that is not on the list.
+   Then the model check and the write guard run, as today.
+8. The commit, and a history line on the record (who asked, when, their words). The end sequence
+   lands the change.
+
+The shaping steps (the gather, the questions, the shape, the reuse) do not run in an edit.
+
+## What goes in the play's ICE (through /edit-play)
+
+- A constraint: an edit starts only from a person's `--edit` words; it changes only the parts on
+  the list the person saw and approved; every other line stays word for word.
+- A failure condition: a change outside the list, an edit with no yes, or an edit of something the
+  rule never edits.
+- Its recovery: put back each change outside the list; nothing is kept without the yes.
+- A scenario: an edit of two "who fixes" lines changes those two lines and nothing else.
+- `--redraft` is removed from the play, its script and its test.
+
+## The shared part (#905) that --edit needs
+
+- The rule page: what an edit is, the command, what is never edited (eval files, accepted ADRs,
+  code), the history line.
+- The skill that turns the person's words into the exact list, shared by every play in #904.
+- The two scripts, shared like the other shared scripts: apply the list, and check before against
+  after.
+- An ADR, through /record-decision.
+
+## Where it is built
+
+On the #798 branch, in the same change (PR #900): `/shape-slice` exists only on the plan 788 line,
+not on main, so `--edit` replaces `--redraft` where it is. The six "who fixes" lines already fixed
+stay as they are.
diff --git a/.garura/project/issues/798/specs/edit-design.md b/.garura/project/issues/798/specs/edit-design.md
--- /dev/null
+++ b/.garura/project/issues/798/specs/edit-design.md
@@ -0,0 +1,44 @@
+# Design draft: one standard way for a play to edit a record that already exists
+
+Status: draft for the product owner; nothing is built. 2026-10-27, from the PR #900 round 3 review.
+
+## The problem
+
+Every play that writes the product model or the work items is add-only by design ("keeps what is
+there"), so it never overwrites what a person confirmed. When a person wants to change a record
+that already exists, no play can. Change #798 hit this four times: the proof line of intent #788
+(#901), two spine one-lines, the slice's name, and six "who fixes" lines of the hidden file. Each
+time it was patched a different way: a new rule in one play, a script change, a hand edit, and the
+`--redraft` option that the review raised to P1.
+
+## The proposal
+
+1. One word for it: **edit**. Garura's names already use "edit" for "change a thing that exists"
+   (`/edit-play`, `/edit-loop`, `/edit-ontology`). "refactor" means a new shape with the same
+   meaning, which is the wrong word for a content change. "redraft" names how the text is made
+   and suggests the whole record is rewritten.
+2. How a person asks: the play's usual command, then `edit:` and their words, for example
+   `/discover-intent 788 edit: the proof line should say …`. The play's skill turns the words into
+   an exact list of what changes (record, section, line) and shows that list back.
+3. The rules every such play follows, kept in one shared rule file:
+   - Only a person starts an edit. A play never edits a confirmed record on its own.
+   - An edit changes only what the person named; every other line stays word for word. One shared
+     script compares before and after and refuses any other change.
+   - Show before write: one brief with each changed line, old and new, and each other record the
+     edit makes out of date (named, not changed). Nothing is written before the person's yes.
+   - The record keeps a history line: who asked, when, and their words.
+   - Never edited: eval files (a fixed yardstick, only added to), accepted ADRs (a new ADR
+     supersedes them), and code.
+4. Plays that get it: `/discover-intent` (business intents, #901), `/discover-domains` (domains),
+   `/discover-capabilities` (capabilities, features and their ICE), `/shape-slice` (the slice
+   record and the hidden file's entries), `/shape-plan` (the plan file, the epic and story text).
+   Not `/shape-eval`, not `/record-decision`.
+5. How it is built: an ADR through `/record-decision`; the shared rule and script in the standards,
+   promoted to Phoenix memory; each play through its ICE and `/edit-play` (one constraint, one
+   failure condition, one recovery, one scenario); `/create-play` learns it for new plays.
+
+## What happens in #798
+
+- Take back the `--redraft` commit (d756e304). The six "who fixes" lines (8fceddab) stay; the
+  product owner approved them.
+- The edit design is its own work, filed on its own, not inside #798.
